{:nav-title "Anatomy of a query"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Anatomy of a Query"]
  [:p "You have already run a query. Before writing more of them, let's name their parts, "
   "so you can read any query and reason about it. This is the query from the first page, "
   "with each part labelled:"]
  [:ui/code "clojure"
   "[:find ?name                       ;; what to return
 :where                            ;; what the data must match
 [?entity :pokemon/type \"Grass\"]   ;; pattern 1
 [?entity :pokemon/name ?name]]    ;; pattern 2"]

  [:ul
   [:li [:code ":find"] " says what to return. Here, one value per result: " [:code "?name"] "."]
   [:li [:code ":where"] " lists the " [:strong "data patterns"] " the database has to match."]
   [:li "A " [:strong "data pattern"] " has the shape " [:code "[entity attribute value]"]
    ", one fact about one entity, like the " [:code ":pokemon/name"] " of Bulbasaur in the previous chapter. "
    "Each slot holds either a fixed value, "
    "like " [:code ":pokemon/type"] " or " [:code "\"Grass\""] ", or a variable."]
   [:li "A " [:strong "logic variable"] " starts with " [:code "?"] ". It is a name for whatever fits, "
    "and it has to mean " [:strong "the same thing everywhere it appears"] " in the query."]]

  [:h2 "Read it aloud"]
  [:p "Read a query as a sentence: " [:em "\"Find the " [:code "?name"] " of every " [:code "?entity"]
                                      " that has the type Grass and that has the name " [:code "?name"] ".\""]
   " The first pattern picks the Grass entities, and the second reads their names."]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?entity :pokemon/type "Grass"]
    [?entity :pokemon/name ?name]]]

  [:h2 "Sharing a variable is a join"]
  [:p "Both patterns above use " [:code "?entity"] ", so they describe the same entity. "
   "Datomic finds the entities that match the first pattern and keeps the ones that also match the second. "
   "If you know SQL, this is what " [:code "JOIN ... ON"] " does, "
   "and here the shared variable is all it takes."]
  [:p "Let's use it to find the Pokemon that are both Fire and Flying. "
   "Two patterns on " [:code ":pokemon/type"] ", one variable:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/type "Fire"]
    [?e :pokemon/type "Flying"]
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]]]
  [:p "Now give the second pattern a variable of its own. "
   [:code "?flyer"] " is not " [:code "?fire"] ", so the two patterns are unrelated. "
   "The second one only asks whether some Flying Pokemon exists, and one does, "
   "so it filters nothing. Every Fire Pokemon comes back:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name
    :where
    [?fire :pokemon/type "Fire"]
    [?flyer :pokemon/type "Flying"]
    [?fire :pokemon/name ?name]]]
  [:p "If two patterns are about the same thing, they have to share a variable."]

  [:h2 "Two more things to know"]
  [:ul
   [:li "The patterns in " [:code ":where"] " are combined with AND. "
    "A result has to satisfy every one of them."]
   [:li "The result is a collection of tuples with no duplicates and no promised order. "
    "Two Pokemon that share the values you return give one row, not two. That matters when we count "
    "in the " [:a {:href "/aggregations"} "Aggregations"] " chapter."]]

  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Find the Pokemon that are both Water and Ice. "
    "Then rename the variable in one of the type patterns, and predict how the result changes before you run it."]]

  [:div {:class "recap"}
   [:span {:class "recap-label"} "You can now"]
   [:ul
    [:li "Name every part of a query: " [:code ":find"] ", " [:code ":where"]
     ", data patterns, and logic variables."]
    [:li "Explain why sharing a variable between two patterns performs a join."]
    [:li "Know that " [:code ":where"] " clauses are combined with AND, "
     "and that results are an unordered, deduplicated set of tuples."]]]]}
